  "insider_note": "Ten steps in the book, four marks on the paper. Group them: attract, attach, curl, thread, divide, differentiate, connect. Numbering the steps is what makes this answer readable.",
  "answer": {
    "page_header": [
      "Section B — Short Answer Question",
      "Mineral Nutrition (Botany-II) · 4 marks"
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_attract",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Attraction and attachment",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Attraction",
        "margin_note": "Four numbered steps. Name Rhizobium and name the legume host.",
        "lines": [
          "1. The roots of the host legume release sugars",
          "and amino acids into the soil.",
          "2. These attract Rhizobium bacteria.",
          "3. The bacteria multiply and colonise the soil",
          "around the root.",
          "4. They attach to the epidermis of the root",
          "hair cells."
        ],
        "why": "The legume calls the bacteria in rather than being invaded, which is why the association is a partnership and not an infection in the ordinary sense.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying the bacteria find the root by chance. The root RELEASES chemicals that attract them.",
          "Naming the wrong bacterium. It is Rhizobium, in the roots of LEGUMES."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "The root advertises with sugar; the bacteria answer the advertisement."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_thread",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Curling and the infection thread",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Infection thread",
        "margin_note": "Curl, thread, destination. The words 'infection thread' are the mark.",
        "lines": [
          "5. The root hair CURLS, forming a hook.",
          "6. The bacteria invade the curled root hair.",
          "7. An INFECTION THREAD is produced.",
          "8. The thread carries the bacteria inward,",
          "into the cortex."
        ],
        "why": "The bacteria never travel free through the plant; the thread is the tube that carries them in, which is why the curl has to form first.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Leaving out the infection thread. It is the named structure of this mark.",
          "Saying the bacteria enter the xylem. They enter the CORTEX."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Curl, then thread, then cortex: the bacteria never travel loose."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_nodule",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Nodule formation",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Nodule",
        "margin_note": "Division, differentiation, nodule. Three lines.",
        "lines": [
          "9. The bacteria in the cortical cells stimulate",
          "the host cells to DIVIDE.",
          "10. This produces specialised nitrogen fixing",
          "cells.",
          "11. These cells form the root nodule."
        ],
        "why": "The nodule is built from the plant's own dividing cells, not from bacterial tissue, which is why it is an organ of the root rather than a growth on it.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying the bacteria build the nodule. The HOST cells divide and build it.",
          "Calling the nodule a disease swelling. It is a specialised nitrogen fixing organ."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "The bacteria give the signal; the plant builds the room."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_vascular",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Vascular connection",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Connection",
        "margin_note": "Close on the vascular connection and say what moves each way.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "A direct vascular link, so nutrients can be",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          {
            "text": "exchanged both ways",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "12. The mature nodule establishes a direct",
          "vascular connection with the host.",
          "This allows the exchange of nutrients: the",
          "plant supplies food, the bacteria supply fixed",
          "nitrogen."
        ],
        "why": "Without a vascular link the fixed nitrogen would be trapped in the nodule, so the connection is what makes the whole arrangement useful to the plant.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Ending at 'nodule is formed'. The vascular connection is the last mark.",
          "Saying only the plant benefits. Both partners exchange nutrients."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "No pipe, no profit: the vascular link is what makes it a partnership."
      },
